EDIT 1 — Cite the contrasting anchoring null (Jaarsma et al.) alongside Sheppert
Justification: Jaarsma EH, Ring D, Wickman J, Drost A. "Can Large Language Models
Preserve Diagnostic Accuracy Despite Patient Self-Diagnosis and Framing Bias in
Hypothetical Upper-Extremity Scenarios?" J Hand Surg Am 2026.
doi 10.1016/j.jhsa.2026.07.014, PMID 42640232. Peer-reviewed; verified via PubMed
27 Aug 2026. Finding: ChatGPT (GPT-5), 180 randomized vignettes across five benign
upper-extremity musculoskeletal conditions; 92% diagnostic accuracy UNAFFECTED by
patient-stated self-diagnosis; the model deviated from the proposed self-diagnosis
in 65% of scenarios, 89% of deviations correctly aligning with the intended
diagnosis. Authors conclude "limited susceptibility to the anchoring, confirmation,
and acquiescence biases known to affect human diagnostic reasoning."
Why it must be cited: the Scientific Introduction cites Sheppert et al. (LLMs
anchor ~4x more than physicians) as the quantitative anchoring gap, and this
project's own standard — set with the Ada et al. register null in the PI-approved
21 Aug cycle — is to declare competing nulls rather than omit them. The Jaarsma
null does NOT falsify any protocol claim (different model generation, benign
non-emergency conditions, diagnosis-selection rather than disposition, no
asymmetric-harm axis), but a reviewer who finds it uncited will ask why. Citing it
converts a vulnerability into a stated, falsifiable rationale: anchoring resistance
may be model-generation- and stakes-dependent, which is what the benchmark measures.
Sections affected: Scientific Introduction (anchoring prediction, "Expected
Findings"); Scientific Introduction Key References; PROTOCOL_v3 §0 DECISION 1
evidence note.

